  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Warm conditions continue.\n\nA large ridge will continue to move eastward and settle over the \nGeorgia/South Carolina border by Thursday. The ridge will provide \nwarmer than average temperatures for the region with highs \nprogressively increasing each day until their peak on Thursday. By \nthis day, highs will likely be in the mid-80s to mid-90s with heat \nindex values as high as 100 in the Piedmont. Heat Advisory criteria \nis unlikely to be met but conditions will still be hot for this time \nof year so vulnerable populations are encouraged to take precautions \nto prevent heat related illnesses. An approaching cold front may \ntake longer to arrive and pass through than previously thought \nso warm conditions may still persist into the weekend.\n\n\nKey Message 2: Multiple days of precipitation possible starting by \nThursday.\n\nThe ridge moving eastward will split into two parts with one over \nthe southeastern United States and the other over the southwestern \npart of CONUS. A cold front with an associated mid-level trough will \npass through Thursday night into Friday morning and push our \nridge further east and into Bermuda. The timing of this system's\npassage is still a little questionable and will be monitored in\nfuture forecasts. While wind shear and CAPE will be available \non Friday afternoon and evening, abysmal lapse rates of 5.0-5.5\nC/km will prevent any serious convection and storm \norganization. Rain is still possible and areas to the west are \nlikely to receive the most as the cold front moves through. As \nthe mid-level ridge is pushed out, a trough takes advantage of \nthe gap between the ridges and forms to our west and passes \nthrough the region during the weekend. This wave and the energy \nit supplies will likely keep precipitation chances going \nthroughout the weekend. While severe chances overall are low for\nthis whole period, flooding is a concern if there are repeated \nwaves of rain over the same area. PWATs are expected to increase\nto anomalously high values between 1.5-1.75\". Some \ndeterministic models do not rule out high end values of up to \n2\" which would be double than what is average for this time of \nyear. The forecast for this weekend has seen several edits so \nstay tuned for additional updates on rainfall totals and \npotential flood hazards.",
